\caption{The system prompt $S$ and the output $y$ are drawn in bold. $S$ enters the generator and has no arrow into the retriever, which is what allows a change in $y$ to be attributed to a change in $S$.}
\label{fig:causal}
\end{figure}

For the effect of the prompt to be interpretable, the following are held constant across conditions. Each query is used unchanged in every condition it is run in, so that every comparison is made within a query rather than between queries, and the knowledge base is fixed at $D = D_0$. The retriever and its parameters are fixed at $\eta = \eta_0$, and the language model at $\theta = \theta_0$, so the model is neither retrained nor replaced between conditions.  Generation is prompt-dependent: although $\theta$ stays fixed, the conditional distribution it defines may differ between $S_0$ and $S_j$. Finally, decoding is stochastic, so the same configuration can produce different outputs from one run to the next; the term $\varepsilon$ carries that variation:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:noise}
Y = G(q,z,S;\theta,\varepsilon),
\end{equation}

which is why each condition is repeated several times in Chapter 5.

\section{The Causal Effect}

Setting the system prompt to a fixed value is an intervention, written $\mathrm{do}(S = S_j)$ following \citet{pearl2009causality}. The notation separates \textit{setting} a variable from merely \textit{observing} it. For a query $q$ under prompt condition $S_j$, the output of the system is the random variable
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:potential}
Y \sim p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S_j).
\end{equation}

For one query the effect of the prompt is the difference in the expected value of the measured property $M(Y)$ between the two conditions. That difference is not, however, the quantity of interest. A prompt that improves the answer to one question and degrades the answer to the next has established no effect at all, and a difference observed on a single input cannot be separated from the particular difficulty, phrasing or subject matter of that input. The research question asks about the behaviour of the system over the queries it will actually meet, so the effect has to be defined as an average over the distribution from which queries are drawn. Writing $P_{\mathcal{Q}}$ for that distribution over the query space $\mathcal{Q}$, the causal effect of prompt condition $S_j$ relative to the baseline $S_0$ is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:estimand}
\tau_j \;=\; \mathbb{E}_{q \sim P_{\mathcal{Q}}}\Bigl[\; \mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(\cdot \mid q,D,S_j)}\bigl[M(Y)\bigr] \;-\; \mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(\cdot \mid q,D,S_0)}\bigl[M(Y)\bigr] \;\Bigr],
\end{equation}

which connects the probability model of Section~\ref{sec:ragdist} directly to the quantity the experiment estimates. Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} is what Chapter 5 measures.

Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} is an average treatment effect in the standard sense: the query plays the role of the unit, the prompt condition the role of the treatment, and $\tau_j$ the mean over units of the difference between the two treatments. Two features of how it is written matter. The difference is taken inside the outer expectation rather than between two separately averaged quantities. That is what makes each query its own control, and it matches the paired design of Section~\ref{sec:intervention}. By linearity of expectation the two forms agree, but only the form given here records the pairing. The expectations are also nested rather than pooled: the inner expectation averages over the randomness of retrieval and decoding at a fixed query, while the outer averages over the queries themselves, so a large effect confined to a handful of inputs is not mistaken for a general one.

The distribution $P_{\mathcal{Q}}$ is not known and cannot be written down. Chapter 5 replaces it with the empirical distribution of a finite query set. That is the point at which the choice of data becomes a claim about generality rather than a matter of convenience, and it is why Section~\ref{sec:knowledgebase} draws the queries from established question-answer datasets rather than composing them for the experiment.

The choice of $M$ is left open here. Section~\ref{sec:goodanswer} describes what that property has to capture, and Section~\ref{sec:metrics} fixes it.

\section{Grounding the Knowledge Base in Established Datasets}
\label{sec:knowledgebase}

The knowledge base has so far been written as a set $D = \{d_1,\ldots,d_N\}$. Filling $D$ is a methodological decision rather than a further step of formalisation. It is settled here because the effect defined in Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} is only as informative as the corpus and the queries it is estimated over.

The  route taken here is to instantiate $D$ and the query set from established question-answer datasets. Three properties recommend it. The questions are naturally occurring rather than authored for the experiment. The corpora are public, versioned and split in a documented way, so a third party can repeat the experiment. And because published results for a wide range of retrieval and generation systems already exist on these datasets, the effect estimated in Chapter 5 can be read against a background of prior work instead of standing alone.

The collection is built from queries sampled from Bing search logs, with candidate passages retrieved for each query and free-form answers written by human judges \citep{nguyen2016msmarco}. MS MARCO provides the queries $q$, the documents that populate $D$, and, through its annotations, both the reference answer $a_q$ and the fact set against which an output can be judged. 

What it does not provide is a definition of a good answer. An annotation records which answer is correct; it says nothing about whether an answer is grounded in the evidence actually retrieved, whether it addresses the question directly rather than evading it, or whether it obeys the requirements the system prompt states. Those properties are the object of this thesis, and defining them is the subject of the next section.
\section{What Counts as a Good Answer}
\label{sec:goodanswer}
Quality in a retrieval-augmented system is not one property but several, and they can fail independently of one another. The first is groundedness. An answer should rest on the documents the retriever actually supplied. The second is relevance. An answer should address the question that was asked and address it directly, so that hedging, padding and drift towards a neighbouring question all count as failures. The third is correctness. An answer should agree with what is in fact the case, which is a different demand from the first and is treated separately here for a reason given below. The fourth is obedience. Whatever the system prompt asks for should be visible in the output itself, whether in length, in form, or in the handling of missing evidence, since a prompt that is not followed cannot be said to have any effect on quality at all.

All four are properties of the text the generator produces. Each of the four is estimated by a measure taken from published work. Groundedness by faithfulness, which asks whether every claim can be traced to a retrieved passage; relevance by answer relevance, that asks whether the response addresses the question put to it; correctness by agreement with the reference answer recorded in the data; and obedience by whether the output satisfies every instruction the prompt states.

The formal definitions this evaluation rests on are introduced in Chapter 5. That chapter states the set of acceptable answers precisely and fixes faithfulness, answer relevance, correctness and instruction adherence as the four measures that estimate the four properties. Each of them is used in turn as the measured property $M$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand}, so the effect of a prompt condition on each is estimated separately. Chapter 5 also specifies the corpus, the query set, the experimental conditions and the procedure by which those effects are estimated.

\chapter{Experimental Setup}

Chapter 4 built a model of the RAG system and defined the effect of a prompt condition as the average treatment effect $\tau_j$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand}. This chapter turns that model into a concrete experiment. It states the research question and the hypothesis, describes the system that is tested and which of its parts are held fixed, defines the prompt conditions, explains how the data are collected, and fixes how the outputs are scored and analysed. All experiments are implemented in Python. The implementation follows the structure of this chapter, and every design decision stated here is checked by the code before any data are collected.

\section{Research Question and Hypothesis}
\label{sec:goal}

The experiment asks one question: \textit{does a RAG system behave differently when the structure of its system prompt is changed while the meaning of the prompt stays the same?}

Structure means how the prompt is written: which words are used, how the sentences are built, and how the text is laid out on the page. Meaning means the set of requirements $\Sigma$ that the prompt states, in the sense of Equation~\eqref{eq:prompt}. Every condition of the experiment keeps $\Sigma$ fixed and changes only the wording $\varphi$. If the system responded only to what a prompt requires and not to how it is written, all wordings of one prompt would produce the same distribution of answers. Whether they do is the empirical question. Stated as hypotheses:

\begin{description}
    \item[$H_0$.] System prompts that state the same requirements in different wordings produce the same distribution of answer quality. For every baseline $b$ and every wording $j$, $\tau_j = 0$.
    \item[$H_1$.] At least one rewording changes answer quality: $\tau_j \neq 0$ for some $j$.
\end{description}

The comparison is always between a reworded prompt $S_{b,j}$ and the canonical prompt $S_{b,0}$ of the same baseline, on the same queries and with the same retrieved evidence. What is measured is the change in answer quality, where quality is the four properties of Section~\ref{sec:goodanswer}: grounded, relevant, correct and obedient.

One secondary question is asked of the same data. Does the answer to the main question survive when the quality of the evidence changes? The evidence is therefore supplied at two levels, as a controlled factor rather than as a further treatment (Section~\ref{sec:variables}).

\section{Overview of the Experiment}
\label{sec:overview}

The system under test is the single-shot RAG architecture of Chapter 2. It is used for the reason given in Section~\ref{sec:advanced}: in this architecture the system prompt reaches the answer along one path only, through the generator, so its effect can be identified. A query is embedded, the $K$ most similar passages are retrieved from a vector index, and a language model produces the answer from the system prompt, the passages and the query. Table~\ref{tab:fixed} lists the components of that system and the value each one is fixed at.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{The components of the system under test and their fixed values. None of them changes between prompt conditions.}
\label{tab:fixed}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Component & Fixed value \\
\midrule
Knowledge base $D$ & MS MARCO v2.1 validation passages, $N = 122{,}678$ \\
Chunking $\chi$ & one passage per chunk, as published \\
Embedding model & \texttt{all-mpnet-base-v2}, mean pooling, L2-normalised \\
Vector index & FAISS flat inner-product index, exact search \\
Retrieval depth $K$ & 10 \\
Generator $\theta$ & \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} via the chat completions API \\
Decoding & temperature $1.0$ (the API default), at most 256 new tokens \\
Judge & \texttt{gpt-4.1-mini}, temperature $0$, fixed prompts \\
Query set & 100 MS MARCO queries, seed 20260904 \\
Repetitions $n$ & 3 per cell \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The experiment has three stages. In the first stage, retrieval is run once for every query and its result is written to disk. In the second stage, every prompt condition is applied to every query, reading the stored retrieval result, and the answers are written to disk. In the third stage, the answers are scored and the prompt effect is estimated. Section~\ref{sec:procedure} describes the first two stages in detail and Section~\ref{sec:metrics} the third.

\section{System-Prompt Conditions}
\label{sec:conditions}

The system prompt is the only thing that changes between conditions. A condition is a pair of a baseline and a wording. This section first introduces the three baselines and then the four wordings each baseline is written in.

\subsection{Three Published Baselines}

The prompts are not written for this experiment. Each baseline is a system prompt published with a retrieval-augmented system, and its canonical form is reproduced here exactly as it appears in its source. The three sources state different numbers of requirements, and that is why three are used rather than one. A prompt with one requirement gives a change of wording little to act on. A prompt with ten requirements is long, and a wording effect found on it alone could be an effect of length. Finding the same wording effect on all three separates the effect of wording from the effect of how much the prompt asks for.

Baseline $A$ is the response-generation prompt of \citet{friel2024ragbench}. It states one requirement: answer from the supplied context.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={Baseline $A$: the response-generation prompt of \citet{friel2024ragbench}.},label={lst:minimal}]
Use the following pieces of context to answer the question.
{documents}
Question: {question}
\end{lstlisting}

Baseline $B$ is the VANILLA instruction of \citet{gao2023alce}. It states the most. Beyond grounding, it constrains accuracy, engagement, concision and register, it fixes the syntax and the density of citations, and it warns that some of the supplied results may be irrelevant.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={Baseline $B$: the VANILLA instruction of \citet{gao2023alce}, reproduced from their Table 23.},label={lst:alce}]
Write an accurate, engaging, and concise answer for the given
question using only the provided search results (some of which
might be irrelevant) and cite them properly. Use an unbiased and
journalistic tone. Always cite for any factual claim. When citing
several search results, use [1][2][3]. Cite at least one document
and at most three documents in each sentence. If multiple documents
support the sentence, only cite a minimum sufficient subset of the
documents.
\end{lstlisting}

Baseline $C$ is the instruction of \citet{mao2024fitrag}. Its authors describe it as three parts: the first directs the model to the retrieved passages, the second asks it to read the question carefully, and the third asks for an answer together with a justification. They report that this form outperforms both a bare instruction and a chain-of-thought variant on the same passages.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={Baseline $C$: the comprehensive instruction of \citet{mao2024fitrag}.},label={lst:fitrag}]
Refer to the passage below and answer the following question.
Make sure you fully understand the meaning of the question and
passages. Then give the answer and explain why you choose this
answer.
Passages: {passages}
Question: {question}
\end{lstlisting}

The published prompts contain placeholders for the documents and the question. In this experiment the instruction text alone forms the system prompt $S$ and is sent in the system role of the chat interface. The passages and the question are sent in the user role, in the fixed template of Listing~\ref{lst:userturn}. The template is the same in every condition, so the only text that differs between conditions is $S$. Passages are numbered from one, so that a citation instruction has something to refer to and so that the position of a passage can be recovered from the answer.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={The user turn. It is identical in every condition; only the system turn $S$ changes. With no passages, the line after \texttt{Passages:} reads \texttt{(none)}.},label={lst:userturn}]
Passages:
[1] <first passage>
[2] <second passage>
...

Question: <the query>
Answer:
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Four Wordings of Each Baseline}

Each baseline is written in four wordings. The first is the canonical form above. The other three each change one aspect of the wording and leave everything else as it is. The axes follow the paraphrase types of \citet{wahle2024paraphrase}, \citet{zhan2024lexical} and \citet{kostic2026samemeaning}, as explained in Section~\ref{sec:promptreq}. Table~\ref{tab:wordings} states what each wording changes and the rule under which it was written.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{The four wordings. Every wording of a baseline states the same requirement set $\Sigma_b$.}
\label{tab:wordings}
\begin{tabular}{llp{7.6cm}}
\toprule
$j$ & Wording & What changes, and the rule kept \\
\midrule
0 & canonical & Nothing. The published form. \\
1 & lexical & Words are replaced by synonyms. Clause order and voice are kept. \\
2 & syntactic & Clauses are reordered and active voice becomes passive. The vocabulary is kept as far as English allows. Obligation is carried uniformly by \textit{is to be}, so that the change of voice does not change how strongly something is required. \\
3 & format & The same wording is laid out as a list with headings instead of as prose. List items are bare noun phrases, so that no obligation is added or removed. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The rules in the last column matter. A rewording that changed how strongly an instruction is stated, for example by turning \textit{always cite} into \textit{please try to cite}, would change what the prompt requires. Such a change is a change of $\Sigma$, and a difference in behaviour caused by it could not be read as an effect of structure. No condition of this experiment makes a change of that kind.

The four wordings of baseline $A$ are given in Listing~\ref{lst:baselineA} in Chapter 4. Listings~\ref{lst:baselineB} and \ref{lst:baselineC} give the three rewordings of baselines $B$ and $C$; their canonical forms are Listings~\ref{lst:alce} and \ref{lst:fitrag} above.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={The lexical ($B1$), syntactic ($B2$) and format ($B3$) wordings of baseline $B$.},label={lst:baselineB}]
B1  Compose a precise, compelling, and succinct reply for the posed
    query drawing only on the supplied search results (a number of
    which may be immaterial) and reference them correctly. Adopt an
    impartial and journalistic register. Invariably reference every
    factual assertion. When referencing several search results,
    employ [1][2][3]. Reference at minimum one document and at
    maximum three documents per sentence. If several documents
    corroborate the sentence, only reference a minimal adequate
    subset.

B2  For the given question an accurate, engaging, and concise answer
    is to be written from the provided search results alone, some
    of which might be irrelevant, and cited properly. An unbiased,
    journalistic tone is to be used. A citation is always given for
    any factual claim. Where several results are cited, [1][2][3]
    is used. In each sentence at least one and at most three
    documents are cited. Where multiple documents support a
    sentence, only a minimum sufficient subset is cited.

B3  ANSWER
    - accurate, engaging, and concise, for the given question
    - source: only the provided search results
    - note: some of the provided results might be irrelevant

    CITATIONS
    - cite the search results properly
    - always cite for any factual claim
    - when citing several search results: [1][2][3]
    - in each sentence: at least one document, at most three documents
    - if multiple documents support the sentence: only a minimum
      sufficient subset

    TONE
    - unbiased and journalistic
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={The lexical ($C1$), syntactic ($C2$) and format ($C3$) wordings of baseline $C$.},label={lst:baselineC}]
C1  Consult the excerpt below and respond to the following query.
    Ensure you completely grasp the sense of the query and excerpts.
    Then supply the response and justify why you select this
    response.

C2  The following question is to be answered from the passage below.
    The meaning of the question and passages is first understood.
    The answer is then given, with an explanation of why it is
    chosen.

C3  SOURCE: the passage below, and nothing else
    READ: fully understand the meaning of the question and passages
    ANSWER: give the answer to the following question
    EXPLAIN: say why you choose this answer
\end{lstlisting}

Prompt length is held nearly constant within a baseline, so that a difference between two wordings cannot be an effect of how much text the prompt contains. Table~\ref{tab:lengths} gives the word counts. No wording departs from its canonical form by more than one tenth, and the code refuses to run if an edit to a prompt breaks that bound.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Word count of every condition, with the deviation from the canonical form of its baseline.}
\label{tab:lengths}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
Baseline & canonical & lexical & syntactic & format \\
\midrule
$A$ & 10 & 10 ($\pm 0\%$) & 11 ($+10\%$) & 11 ($+10\%$) \\
$B$ & 74 & 71 ($-4\%$) & 80 ($+8\%$) & 77 ($+4\%$) \\
$C$ & 33 & 32 ($-3\%$) & 34 ($+3\%$) & 32 ($-3\%$) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Twelve conditions result: three baselines in four wordings each. They are written $S_{b,j}$ with $b \in \{A,B,C\}$ and $j \in \{0,1,2,3\}$. Every comparison is made within a baseline. Comparing $S_{B,1}$ with $S_{A,0}$ would mix a change of wording with a change of what is asked for, so each rewording is compared only with the canonical form of its own baseline.

\section{Variables and Controlled Factors}
\label{sec:variables}

The experiment has one independent variable, one dependent variable, two controlled factors that are varied for robustness, and a set of components that are held constant.

\paragraph{Independent variable.} The wording $j$ of the system prompt, with four levels, nested within the baseline $b$. Changing $j$ changes the structure of the prompt and nothing else. The requirement set $\Sigma_b$ is the same at every level of $j$.

\paragraph{Dependent variable.} The quality of the answer, measured by the four measures of Section~\ref{sec:metrics}: faithfulness, answer relevance, correctness and instruction adherence. Each is used in turn as the measured property $M$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand}, so the effect of a rewording is estimated four times, once per measure, and the four are never combined into a single score. Pooling them would hide direction: a rewording that improved grounding and damaged relevance by the same amount would average to no effect at all.

\paragraph{Controlled factors.} Two factors are varied so that the main question can be asked under more than one condition of the evidence. Neither is a treatment. The prompt effect is estimated separately at each level of each factor, and the levels are not compared with one another as a result of their own.

The first factor is the \textbf{evidence level}. The context $C$ handed to the generator is set directly, as the intervention $\mathrm{do}(C = c_{ik})$ of Chapter 4, at two levels:
\begin{description}
    \item[$c^{\text{gold}}$.] Only the passages the annotation marks as supporting the answer. The evidence needed for the answer is certainly present, so a failure here belongs to the generator rather than to retrieval. Every query is usable at this level, which makes it the more sensitive of the two.
    \item[$c^{\text{ret}}$.] The $K$ passages the fixed retriever actually returns, gold and distractors alike. This is the system as it would be deployed.
\end{description}
Both levels are produced by setting the context, never by changing the corpus and retrieving again, so the index and the retriever are identical for both.

There is no second factor. The retrieval depth is held constant at $K = 10$. That depth admits more of the annotated evidence than a shallower one, as Table~\ref{tab:gate} shows, so more queries are usable at the retrieved level and the comparison there rests on a larger sample; and ten passages give the prompt a longer and noisier context to govern than five would. A second depth was considered as a further robustness factor, but it would have doubled the size of the run to answer a question secondary to the one being asked, and it is not part of this design.

\paragraph{Held constant.} Everything in Table~\ref{tab:fixed}. The generator is one model behind one API, with a fixed temperature and a fixed output limit. Queries and passages are encoded by one embedding model, applied to both sides in the manner of the dense retriever of \citet{karpukhin2020dpr}. The index, built with the library of \citet{johnson2021faiss}, is constructed once and never rebuilt. Each passage is one chunk. Because the embedding model, the index, the chunking and the depth are all fixed, the set $\mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:topk} depends only on the query and the depth and never on the system prompt. The context is held identical in a stronger sense than that. Not only the set of passages but also their order, and at the corrupted level the substituted facts, are the same in every condition: they are determined by the query, the evidence level, the depth and the repetition, and by nothing else. Two conditions compared on the same query therefore receive a character-for-character identical user turn, and the only text that differs between them is the system prompt. Any change in the answer originates at the generator, which is the localisation the intervention of Section~\ref{sec:intervention} assumes.

\paragraph{Covariates.} Two properties of each query are recorded and can be conditioned on in the analysis: the category (direct or multi-hop) and MS MARCO's intent type. Both are defined in Section~\ref{sec:corpus}.

\paragraph{The design.} An observation is one answer, written
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:design}
y_{ijkr} \;\sim\; p_{\mathrm{RAG}}\bigl(y \;\big|\; q_i,\ \mathrm{do}(C = c_{ik}),\ \mathrm{do}(S = S_j)\bigr), \qquad r = 1,\ldots,n,
\end{equation}
for query $i$, prompt condition $j$, evidence level $k$ and repetition $r$. The twelve prompt conditions and the two evidence levels are fully crossed, which gives 24 cells. Every cell is run for all 100 queries and repeated $n = 3$ times, which is 7,200 generations before the retrieval gate of Section~\ref{sec:procedure} removes any.

Within a cell the only remaining source of variation is the decoding randomness $\varepsilon$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:noise}. Each repetition is an independent draw from the same distribution, so averaging $n$ of them leaves the quantity being estimated unchanged and reduces the standard error of the cell mean by a factor of $\sqrt{n}$. Three repetitions is fewer than the five \citet{zhuo2024prosa} use for the same purpose, and the cost of that choice is stated in Section~\ref{sec:limits}: a cell mean taken over three draws is noisier than one taken over five, so a real effect is harder to separate from the noise of sampling.

\section{Knowledge Base and Query Set}
\label{sec:corpus}

Following Section~\ref{sec:knowledgebase}, both the knowledge base and the query set come from MS MARCO \citep{nguyen2016msmarco}, read from the Hugging Face export of version 2.1. The validation split is used. It holds 101,093 queries, of which 12,467 carry a non-empty \texttt{wellFormedAnswers} field. Only those rows are used, because the well-formed answer is the reference string against which correctness is measured, and a query without one cannot be scored.

\paragraph{The knowledge base.} The corpus is defined by a rule, not by a sample: it holds every passage attached to any of the 12,467 rows. MS MARCO ships about ten candidate passages per query, retrieved by Bing and then judged by annotators, so the corpus contains the gold evidence for every query together with the near-misses that were retrieved alongside it. Identical passage texts are indexed once; 124,316 passages become $N = 122{,}678$ after deduplication. There is no distractor pool to size, and the gold passages of a query compete against the whole corpus. Each passage is one chunk and is used at the length at which it was published. The median passage has about 50 words and three sentences; eight in ten passages have between two and six sentences.

\paragraph{The query set.} $N_Q = 100$ queries are sampled from the same 12,467 rows. They fall into two categories, defined by the annotation rather than by any judgement made here. A row is \textit{direct} when exactly one of its passages is marked \texttt{is\_selected}, and \textit{multi-hop} when two or more are. Fifty queries are drawn from each category.

\begin{description}
    \item[$\mathcal{Q}_{\text{direct}}$.] The answer is carried by a single passage. One passage suffices, so a failure here is a failure to use evidence that was present. This is the noise-robustness testbed of \citet{chen2024rgb}.
    \item[$\mathcal{Q}_{\text{multi-hop}}$.] The human-written answer draws on two or more passages. The annotation identifies which, so the need to combine evidence is recorded rather than asserted. This is the information-integration testbed of \citet{chen2024rgb}.
\end{description}

Within each category the draw is balanced across MS MARCO's five intent types, \textsc{description}, \textsc{entity}, \textsc{location}, \textsc{numeric} and \textsc{person}, ten queries per type. The natural distribution is very uneven: on the well-formed rows \textsc{description} makes up 44\% and \textsc{person} 7\%. An unbalanced draw would leave the rarer types with too few queries to say anything about, and would let an aggregate figure be carried by one type. Balancing makes the type usable as a covariate. The draw uses the seed 20260904 and is reproducible from the same split.

The multi-hop half is drawn from a much smaller pool than the direct half. Of the 12,467 well-formed rows, 12,062 are direct and only 386 are multi-hop; 19 have no passage marked at all and are dropped. The thinnest cell, multi-hop \textsc{person}, holds 27 candidates for the ten drawn. The multi-hop sample is therefore a substantial fraction of what exists, which bounds how much a different seed could change it.

For every query the reference answer $a_q$ is its first well-formed answer, and its gold passages are the ones marked \texttt{is\_selected}. Because the corpus is deduplicated, a gold passage whose text also appears under an earlier row is indexed under that earlier row's identifier; the gold identifiers are rewritten accordingly, so that the retrieval check of the next section looks for an identifier that exists.

\section{Experimental Procedure}
\label{sec:procedure}

The experiment runs in two data-collection stages. Retrieval is finished and written to disk before any prompt condition exists. Generation reads that stored output and never calls the retriever. This separation is what enforces the assumption of Section~\ref{sec:intervention} that the system prompt has no effect on which documents are retrieved: there is no execution path along which a prompt could reach the retriever.

\subsection{Stage 1: Retrieval and the Retrieval Gate}

Queries and passages are encoded with \texttt{all-mpnet-base-v2}, using mean pooling over the last layer and L2 normalisation, so that the inner product of two vectors is their cosine similarity. Inputs are truncated at 384 tokens. The index is a flat inner-product index: every query is compared against every passage, with no approximation. At 122,678 vectors of 768 dimensions the index occupies 0.38~GB and fits in memory, and exactness matters more than speed here, because an approximate index would make retrieval depend on how the index was built rather than on the query alone.

Retrieval is run once, at $K = 10$, for all 100 queries. The identifiers, scores and ranks of the returned passages are written to disk.

The retrieval stage is also where the evidence is checked. A query passes the \textit{retrieval gate} at a depth when every passage its annotation marks as supporting appears among the $K$ returned at that depth. For a direct query that is one passage; for a multi-hop query it is every passage the answer draws on, because an answer that needs two passages cannot be produced from one. Table~\ref{tab:gate} reports the result. It is a property of the fixed retriever and the corpus, not of any prompt condition, and it is reported before the results because it sets the denominator of the retrieved level.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{The retrieval gate at the depth used, $K = 10$. A query passes when all of its gold passages are among the ten returned. Gold recall counts the gold passages that arrived, over all 152 gold passages of the query set. The figures for $K = 5$ are given for comparison; that depth is not run.}
\label{tab:gate}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
$K$ & Passed & direct & multi-hop & Gold recall \\
\midrule
$10$ (used) & 88 / 100 & 45 / 50 & 43 / 50 & 139 / 152 (91.4\%) \\
5 & 74 / 100 & 41 / 50 & 33 / 50 & 123 / 152 (80.9\%) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Queries that fail the gate at a depth are excluded from the retrieved level at that depth. The measures of Section~\ref{sec:metrics} are not defined on evidence that was never supplied: when the context lacks the passage carrying the answer, a correct answer can only have come from the generator's memory, and an incorrect answer cannot be told apart from a faithful reading of the other passages. Scoring such a cell would measure the retriever and report the result as an effect of the prompt. The exclusion is applied to all twelve conditions at once, because retrieval is identical across them, so the pairing survives: every comparison is still made within a query, and no condition is evaluated on queries another condition was not. The gold, corrupted and empty levels set the context directly, cannot fail retrieval, and run over the full query set.

\subsection{Stage 2: Generation}

For every query, every cell of the design is run $n = 3$ times. The cells are visited in a random order per query, so that any drift in the API over the collection period cannot align with a condition. Every generation is an independent request; nothing is reused between requests, and no random seed is sent, so the repetitions are independent draws. The context for a cell is built as follows.

\begin{itemize}
    \item At $c^{\text{gold}}$, the passages are the query's gold passages.
    \item At $c^{\text{ret}}$, the passages are the top $K$ from the stored retrieval output at that depth.
\end{itemize}

The order of the passages within the context is shuffled, and the positions of the gold passages are recorded, because answer accuracy depends on where the relevant passage sits \citep{liu2024lostmiddle}. The shuffle is drawn from a generator seeded on the query, the evidence level, the depth and the repetition, and deliberately not on the prompt condition. That is what makes the context identical across conditions. Because the seed still varies with the repetition, the order differs between the five repetitions of a cell, so the mean of a cell is an average over five arrangements rather than a figure tied to one; and because it does not vary with the condition, that same set of five arrangements is used in every condition. The substitutions at the corrupted level are drawn from the same generator and are identical across conditions for the same reason. The system prompt $S_{b,j}$ is sent in the system role and the passages and question in the user role, in the template of Listing~\ref{lst:userturn}. The generator is \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} at temperature 1.0 with at most 256 new tokens.

For each generation the following are written to disk: the query and its category and type; the condition, evidence level, depth and repetition; the identifiers and order of the passages in the context and the positions of the gold passages; the number of substitutions at the corrupted level; the exact system prompt; the model identifier, the decoding settings and the backend fingerprint the API returned; the answer and its token count; and a timestamp. Nothing needed to reconstruct a generation is left implicit.

\subsection{The Pipeline}

In summary, the experiment proceeds in the following steps.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Download the MS MARCO v2.1 validation split and keep the 12,467 rows with a well-formed answer.
    \item Embed every passage attached to those rows and build the index: 122,678 passages.
    \item Sample the query set: 50 direct and 50 multi-hop queries, ten per intent type, seed 20260904.
    \item Retrieve the top 5 and the top 10 passages for every query and apply the retrieval gate at each depth.
    \item For every query, every one of the 24 cells and every one of the 3 repetitions, build the context, send $S_{b,j}$ and the user turn to the generator, and record the answer.
    \item Score every answer with the four measures of Section~\ref{sec:metrics} and compute its cost.
    \item Estimate the effect of each rewording in every cell, once per measure, with a confidence interval, and correct for multiple comparisons.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Evaluation}
\label{sec:metrics}

Many different texts can express the same answer, so the standard defines a set of acceptable answers $\mathcal{Y}^{*}(q,C)$ rather than one ideal string. An answer belongs to that set when it satisfies four requirements at once:

\begin{description}
    \item[Grounded.] It asserts only what the supplied passages state, and introduces nothing from outside them.
    \item[Relevant.] It addresses the question that was asked, and where the evidence will not support an answer it says so rather than producing something off-target.
    \item[Correct.] It agrees with the reference answer of the dataset annotation.
    \item[Obedient.] It satisfies every requirement the system prompt states.
\end{description}

An answer that breaks none of the four is a good answer. One that breaks several is worse than one that breaks one. Each requirement is estimated by one measure. All four measures are taken from published RAG evaluation work rather than defined here. The first three are the measures that the survey of \citet{yu2024ragevalsurvey} names as the evaluation targets of the generation component: faithfulness relates the response to the retrieved documents, answer relevance relates it to the query, and correctness relates it to the reference answer. Retrieval measures are excluded by construction, because the system prompt cannot change what is retrieved.

\subsection{The Four Measures}

\paragraph{Faithfulness.} Whether every claim in the answer can be traced to a supplied passage. It is scored reference-free, after \citet{es2023ragas}, in two steps. First the judge (Section~\ref{sec:judge}) breaks the answer into atomic factual claims. Then, for each claim, the judge decides whether the passages state it or directly entail it. The breach count $n_1(y)$ is the number of unsupported claims; the reported rate is the supported fraction, because a raw count grows with answer length and prompts change how much a model writes. This is the measure the experiment turns on. Every one of the twelve conditions states a grounding constraint, and faithfulness shows whether that constraint is followed in practice. Because the passages are identical across conditions, a change in faithfulness cannot come from a change in what the generator was given. Faithfulness is undefined at the empty level: with no passages every claim is unsupported by construction, and scoring it there would report the absence of context as a failure of grounding. The measure is marked inapplicable there and contributes nothing.

\paragraph{Answer relevance.} Whether the answer addresses the question actually asked, and directly. A prompt can degrade an answer without making it false, by causing hedging, padding, or drift towards a neighbouring question. Relevance is scored reference-free, after \citet{es2023ragas}: the judge writes three questions that the answer would be a good answer to, and those are compared with the question actually asked by their mean cosine similarity in the space of the retrieval encoder, so that no second embedding space is introduced. A mean below $0.5$ counts as a breach. Abstention is read here. At the empty level nothing supports an answer, so an answer that declines is the responsive one and counts as relevant, while one that supplies unrelated material does not. A refusal is detected by a fixed list of patterns such as \textit{cannot be answered} and \textit{not enough information}, because none of the three baselines mandates a fixed abstention string. When evidence was supplied, a refusal counts as a breach.

\paragraph{Correctness.} Whether the answer agrees with the reference answer $a_q$, in the sense of \citet{chen2024rgb}: the answer is correct when, after lowercasing and removing punctuation and articles, it contains the reference. Exact match is not used, because MS MARCO's well-formed answers are full sentences and an answer that says the same thing in other words would be counted wrong. Containment weakens that demand without removing it. The reference must still appear inside the answer word for word, so a paraphrase is still read as an error. Token-level F1 is therefore reported beside containment as a secondary measure, and exact match is recorded for inspection. F1 compares the two strings word by word and survives a rephrasing that containment does not. Section~\ref{sec:limits} states how far the two come apart on this data and why the first of them keeps its place.

Correctness and faithfulness are separate demands. An answer can repeat a supplied passage accurately and still be wrong about the world, and it can be right about the world while resting on nothing the retriever returned. Measuring both keeps the two apart.

\paragraph{Instruction adherence.} Whether the answer does what the prompt told it to. It is scored as prompt-level strict accuracy in the sense of \citet{zhou2023ifeval}: an answer is adherent only if it satisfies every checked requirement at once, so the score is binary. Only requirements that a rule can decide are checked, which is the principle of verifiable instructions, and a condition is held only to what its own baseline states. For baseline $B$ the rule parses the citation markers and checks that every sentence carries between one and three of them, which is what the prompt asks for. For baseline $C$ the rule checks that the answer contains a justification, marked by words such as \textit{because}, \textit{since} or \textit{therefore}. Baseline $A$ states only grounding, which faithfulness already measures, so its adherence is undefined by construction rather than a free pass. Grounding is never checked inside adherence for any baseline, because counting it there as well would count one failure twice. Requirements that no rule can decide, such as an unbiased tone, are recorded as not checked. The requirement set is the same for all four wordings of a baseline, so adherence is directly comparable across them.

\subsection{The Judge}
\label{sec:judge}

Faithfulness and answer relevance have no reference string, so they are scored by a second language model, as \citet{es2023ragas} and \citet{saadfalcon2024ares} do. The judge is \texttt{gpt-4.1-mini}, a different model from the generator, so that the generator never grades its own output, and it is run at temperature 0 so that a verdict depends on the answer and the passages and not on a random draw. Since this thesis assumes that language models are sensitive to their instructions, the judge cannot be assumed reliable. Three precautions are taken, following \citet{zheng2023judge}, who document position and verbosity biases in this setting. The judge's own prompts are fixed constants and are never varied between conditions. The judge is shown only the answer and the passages, never the condition label, the query identifier or the evidence level; this matters because the format condition is recognisable from its surface alone, and a judge error that varied with condition would bias the estimated effect. And the answers are judged in random order, so that position in the judging sequence cannot correlate with condition.

The judge's agreement with human labelling is measured on about 100 held-out answers, drawn evenly across the twelve conditions with the judge's own verdict withheld from the labeller, and reported as Cohen's kappa. It is reported for each condition separately rather than pooled, because a pooled figure can look reassuring while hiding an error that varies with condition.

\subsection{Estimating the Effect of a Rewording}
\label{sec:analysis}

The quantity reported is the average treatment effect of Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand}. Chapter 4 left the measured property $M$ open. It is fixed here as each of the four measures in turn, so the effect of a rewording is estimated four times, once for faithfulness, once for answer relevance, once for correctness and once for instruction adherence. Every measure is oriented so that a higher value is better, and a positive effect therefore means the rewording improved that measure.

The four are never added together. A single combined score would hide the direction of a change: a rewording that improved grounding and damaged relevance by the same amount would produce no combined effect at all, and the thesis would report that nothing happened when in fact two things did.

For one measure $M$, one evidence level and one baseline $b$, the estimate of the effect of wording $j$ is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:tauhat}
\hat{\tau}_{j} \;=\; \frac{1}{|I|} \sum_{i \in I} \Bigl( \bar{M}_{ij} - \bar{M}_{i0} \Bigr), \qquad \bar{M}_{ij} \;=\; \frac{1}{n} \sum_{r=1}^{n} M\bigl(y_{ijr}\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $I$ is the set of queries in that cell and $\bar{M}_{ij}$ is the mean of the measure over the $n$ repetitions of query $i$ under condition $S_{b,j}$. Equation~\eqref{eq:tauhat} is the sample version of Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand}: the inner average takes the expectation over decoding, and the outer one takes it over queries.

Two properties of that formula matter. The difference is taken inside the sum, so every query is compared with itself and the variation between queries drops out. That is the pairing the design was built for, and it is possible only because every query is run in every condition on an identical context. And the repetitions are averaged before the difference is taken, so one query contributes one number. The repetitions measure decoding noise rather than variation between queries, and treating them as independent observations would make the sample look five times larger than it is.

A measure that does not apply to a generation is skipped rather than counted as satisfied. This affects instruction adherence under baseline $A$, whose only requirement is grounding: $\hat{\tau}$ for adherence is therefore reported for baselines $B$ and $C$ only.

Correctness is estimated twice, once by containment and once by token F1. Containment is the measure Section~\ref{sec:metrics} fixes in advance and it is the one that decides the hypothesis. F1 is secondary and is reported beside it. The two are corrected separately, each within its own family of nine rewordings, and the decision of Section~\ref{sec:decision} reads the four measures named above and nothing else. Choosing between them after seeing the results would make the hypothesis depend on which measure gave the clearer answer, so both are reported and the measure fixed in advance keeps its place.

\subsection{Deciding the Hypothesis}
\label{sec:decision}

$\hat{\tau}_{j}$ is an average over a finite sample, so it is almost never exactly zero even when the true effect is. A point estimate on its own therefore cannot decide between the hypotheses of Section~\ref{sec:goal}. Each estimate is reported with its standard error and a 95\% confidence interval, computed from the same paired differences that produced it. An interval that excludes zero is evidence that the rewording changed that measure; one that contains zero is not.

The hypothesis $H_1$ claims that \textit{at least one} rewording changes answer quality, and it ranges over nine rewordings for each measure. Reading nine intervals and concluding $H_1$ because one of them excluded zero would report chance as a finding: at the 5\% level roughly one interval in twenty excludes zero when nothing is happening. The intervals are therefore corrected by the method of \citet{holm1979} across the nine rewordings within each measure, which is the family the claim ranges over. Both the uncorrected and the corrected verdict are reported, so a reader can see what the correction changed.

$H_0$ is rejected if, after correction, at least one rewording moves at least one measure. If no interval survives correction, $H_0$ is not rejected. That is a weaker statement than showing the effect to be zero, and Section~\ref{sec:limits} states how large an effect this design could have detected.

Alongside the estimates, the mean of each measure in each cell is reported, so that an effect can be read against the level it moves from. A change of two points means something different at a faithfulness of $0.9$ than at one of $0.4$.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limits}

The evaluation rests on a single collection. Every result is conditional on MS MARCO: on its queries, its passages, its relevance judgements and its written answers. Section~\ref{sec:knowledgebase} argues that the topical spread within the collection is wide, but spread within one corpus is not replication across several. A prompt effect found here bounds what may be expected elsewhere; it does not establish it.

The query set is balanced across intent types. That makes the type usable as a covariate, but it detaches the query set from the natural distribution of queries: \textsc{person} and \textsc{location} queries are over-represented relative to MS MARCO and \textsc{description} queries under-represented. The prompt effect $\tau_j$ is unaffected, because it is an average of within-query differences and each query is its own control. Any marginal figure over the query set is a balanced average over the design, not an estimate of what the system would do on the queries a deployment receives.

MS MARCO records what people type into a web search engine. Its queries are short, mostly factoid, and drawn from the domains web search covers. Several of the settings that motivate retrieval in Section~\ref{sec:usecase}, such as clinical decision support and legal question answering, are absent from it, and their documents differ from web passages in length, structure and register. Whether an instruction that governs behaviour here governs it there is left to future work.

The design can detect a medium effect but not a small one. With the 88 queries that pass the retrieval gate at $K = 10$, a paired comparison at the 5\% level has an 80\% chance of detecting a standardised effect of about $0.30$, and about $0.39$ once the correction across the nine rewordings is applied; at the gold level, where all 100 queries are usable, the corresponding figures are $0.28$ and $0.37$. Those thresholds are stated in units of the observed paired differences, and the three repetitions per cell make those differences noisier than five would, so a given true effect is harder to reach them with. If a rewording changes a measure by less than that, this experiment will not distinguish it from decoding noise, and a failure to reject $H_0$ has to be read as that limit rather than as evidence that prompt structure does not matter.

The containment form of correctness cannot see through a paraphrase, and on this data that limit binds hard. MS MARCO's reference answers are full sentences, the generator rarely reproduces one word for word, and a correct answer written in other words scores zero. The measure therefore sits near the bottom of its range in every condition, at a mean of $0.053$ against $0.012$ for exact match, and 849 of the 6,768 generations score zero on it while their token F1 exceeds $0.6$. A measure with that little room to move is insensitive to the size of change this experiment produces, and what it does respond to is phrasing, which is the very thing the rewordings alter. That is why the secondary F1 estimate of Section~\ref{sec:analysis} is reported alongside. Where the two disagree, the disagreement describes the instrument rather than the system: a null on containment says this measure could not detect a change, not that no change occurred. Neither measure establishes truth. F1 rewards overlapping words, so a fluent wrong answer that borrows the vocabulary of the question can score above a short right one.

The generator and the judge are models behind an API. Their weights are not available, and the provider may update a model under the same name. The model identifier, the decoding settings and the backend fingerprint returned by the API are recorded with every generation, so any such change is visible in the data, but an exact repetition of the run depends on the provider keeping the model unchanged.

Two of the four measures are judged by a language model. The precautions of Section~\ref{sec:judge} limit the ways in which the judge can bias the comparison between conditions, and the agreement with human labels bounds its reliability, but they do not remove the dependence on it. Instruction adherence, in turn, checks only the requirements a rule can decide; requirements such as an unbiased tone are stated by the prompts but not scored.


\chapter{Results and Discussion}
\label{ch:results}

\section{What Was Collected}
\label{sec:collected}

The run produced 6,768 answers. At the gold level all 100 queries are usable, which gives $100 \times 12 \times 3 = 3{,}600$ answers. At the retrieved level 88 of the 100 queries pass the gate of Section~\ref{sec:procedure} at $K = 10$, which gives $88 \times 12 \times 3 = 3{,}168$. Every answer was scored on the four measures of Section~\ref{sec:metrics}, and the two judged measures took 30,636 calls to the judge model.

Three checks were run on the collected data before any of it was analysed. Every one of the twelve conditions received a byte-identical user turn for the same query, repetition and evidence level, which is the property Section~\ref{sec:variables} requires and the one the comparison depends on. No answer appears twice. No answer is empty. Together these say that the only thing that differs between two conditions in the same cell is the system prompt.

\section{How the System Behaved Before Any Comparison}
\label{sec:descriptive}

Table~\ref{tab:cellmeans} gives the mean of each measure in each cell. It is worth reading before the effects, because an effect of two points means something different at a faithfulness of $0.92$ than at an adherence of $0.08$.

