Three things stand out. Faithfulness is high everywhere, between $0.912$ and $0.976$. Relevance is stable, between $0.693$ and $0.746$. Both sit in a narrow band, so neither has much room to move. Adherence is the opposite. It runs from $0.083$ to $0.898$, a spread of more than eighty points across conditions that ask for the same thing. Whatever the prompt is doing, this is where it shows.

Correctness measured by containment is near zero in every cell, from $0.015$ to $0.120$. Section~\ref{sec:limits} explains why. The measure asks whether the reference answer appears inside the generated answer word for word, and the generator almost never repeats a full MS MARCO sentence. The secondary F1 estimate in the fourth column is the same comparison made word by word, and it moves in a range four to five times wider.

One pattern in the table runs against expectation. Faithfulness is higher at the retrieved level than at the gold level, in every condition. The gold context contains only the passages annotated as necessary, so it is the easier setting for answering the question, but it is the harder setting for being fully supported: with fewer passages in front of it, more of what the model writes has no source in the context to point at. The retrieved context at $K = 10$ carries more text, and more text gives more claims something to rest on. This is an observation about the data, not something the design was built to test, and it is not part of the hypothesis.

\section{The Effect of Rewording}
\label{sec:effects}

Each rewording is compared with the canonical form of its own baseline, using Equation~\eqref{eq:tauhat}. Every comparison is paired within a query. The results are grouped by measure below, because that is the family the correction of Section~\ref{sec:decision} is applied within.

\subsection{Instruction Adherence}

This is where the effect lives. Table~\ref{tab:adherence} gives all six estimates in both cells. It covers baselines $B$ and $C$ only. Baseline $A$ states one requirement, that the answer come from the supplied context, and faithfulness already measures exactly that, so there is no second rule left for adherence to check. Section~\ref{sec:notshown} returns to what that costs.

