Eleven of the twelve estimates exclude zero after correction. Ten of them do so in both cells, which means the same rewording produced the same change on two different sets of evidence. Only C3 at the retrieved level fails to, and it is the smallest effect in the table.

The sizes are not small. Rewording baseline $C$ with synonyms costs it 44 points of adherence at the gold level. Rewriting the same requirements in the passive voice costs it 46. Baseline $B$ moves the other way: every rewording of it scores higher than the published form, and the lexical rewording gains 22 points at the gold level and 26 at the retrieved level.

\subsection{Faithfulness}

One estimate of nine survives correction: B1 at the gold level, $\hat{\tau} = +0.031$ with a 95\% interval of $[+0.014, +0.047]$. The same comparison at the retrieved level gives $+0.009$ with an interval that contains zero. A result that appears in one cell and not the other is weak evidence. Given the ceiling reported in Section~\ref{sec:descriptive}, where faithfulness never falls below $0.91$, it should not be read as more than that.

\subsection{Answer Relevance}

Nothing survives correction, in either cell. The largest estimate is $+0.012$. Relevance sits between $0.693$ and $0.746$ in every condition and the rewordings do not move it.

\subsection{Correctness}

Measured by containment, nothing survives correction, in either cell. All eighteen intervals contain zero and most of them are wide.

That null belongs to the measure. Table~\ref{tab:f1} gives the same estimates computed on the secondary token F1 measure of Section~\ref{sec:analysis}, on the same answers, with the same estimator and the same correction.

