B1 lexical   & $+0.041$* & $[+0.026, +0.056]$ & $+0.056$* & $[+0.036, +0.077]$ \\
B2 syntactic & $+0.032$* & $[+0.019, +0.045]$ & $+0.032$* & $[+0.015, +0.049]$ \\
B3 format    & $+0.038$* & $[+0.025, +0.051]$ & $+0.045$* & $[+0.027, +0.064]$ \\
C1 lexical   & $+0.011$* & $[+0.003, +0.018]$ & $+0.006$\phantom{*} & $[-0.000, +0.013]$ \\
C2 syntactic & $+0.036$* & $[+0.028, +0.044]$ & $+0.027$* & $[+0.020, +0.034]$ \\
C3 format    & $+0.055$* & $[+0.044, +0.065]$ & $+0.060$* & $[+0.051, +0.068]$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Thirteen of the eighteen estimates exclude zero, and twelve of those are six rewordings that hold in both cells. The two measures are asking about the same property of the same answers and they disagree completely. The disagreement is a statement about the instruments, not about the system. Containment could not detect a change here; that is different from there being none.

\section{The Verdict on the Hypothesis}
\label{sec:verdict}

Sixty-six estimates were made across the four measures fixed in Section~\ref{sec:metrics}. Twelve exclude zero after the correction of Section~\ref{sec:decision}: eleven on adherence and one on faithfulness.

$H_0$ states that prompts stating the same requirements in different wordings produce the same distribution of answer quality, so that $\tau_j = 0$ for every baseline and every wording. That is rejected. $H_1$ claims that at least one rewording changes answer quality, and eleven rewordings do, most of them in both cells.

The rejection does not rest on the single faithfulness result, nor on the secondary correctness measure, neither of which entered the decision. It rests on instruction adherence, where the effects are large, replicated across two evidence levels, and present in both directions.

\section{Reading the Adherence Result}
\label{sec:reading}

An effect of forty points needs an explanation of what changed in the text. This section gives one for each baseline. It describes what the answers look like; it does not add anything to the test above.

\subsection{Baseline B: The Model Cites, But Not in Every Sentence}

Baseline $B$ asks for a citation in every sentence, and the rule of Section~\ref{sec:metrics} follows it strictly: one sentence without a citation fails the whole answer. Under the canonical form only $15.8\%$ of answers pass. That figure is easy to misread. The model does cite. A citation marker appears somewhere in $98.6\%$ of the canonical answers. What it fails to do is cite in every sentence: across all of $B_0$, $53.0\%$ of sentences carry a marker.

The rewordings change that coverage. Under B1 it rises to $65.3\%$. Measured per sentence rather than per answer, the effect of B1 is $+0.153$ at the gold level and $+0.150$ at the retrieved level, both excluding zero. So the improvement in coverage is real.

It is not the whole story, because the strict rule is easier to pass with fewer sentences to get right, and B1 writes fewer. Canonical answers run to $3.1$ sentences on average and B1 answers to $2.5$. Part of the $+0.223$ gain in Table~\ref{tab:adherence} is that shortening, and part is better coverage. The per-sentence estimate separates the two: the coverage gain survives on its own, at about two thirds the size.

\subsection{Baseline C: The Passive Voice Costs Compliance}

Baseline $C$ asks for three things, and the rule checks the one a rule can decide: the answer must explain its choice. Under the canonical form $89.4\%$ of answers do. Under C1 that falls to $50.4\%$ and under C2 to $45.2\%$.

The two prompts that lose compliance are the two that move away from a direct instruction. C0 says \textit{give the answer and explain why you choose this answer}. C1 says \textit{supply the response and justify why you select this response}, which asks for the same thing with different words. C2 says \textit{the answer is then given, with an explanation of why it is chosen}, which states the requirement rather than issuing it. Compliance is lowest under the passive form.

C3 is the one rewording of $C$ that holds. It rewrites the prompt as a labelled list, with \texttt{EXPLAIN:} on its own line, and $84.9\%$ of its answers still explain. It also produces the shortest answers of the four, at 77 output tokens against 117 for the canonical form, so the compliance is not bought with length.

\subsection{The Direction Is Not Fixed}

The most useful thing in Table~\ref{tab:adherence} is that the signs differ. Every rewording of $B$ improves adherence and every rewording of $C$ damages it. Rewording is therefore not a source of degradation that a system should be protected from. It is a variable that can be set well or badly.

It follows that the published form of a prompt is not the best form of it. Baseline $B$ is taken from a paper, and three separate rewritings of it, none of which changes what it asks for, all score higher than the original. A practitioner who copies a prompt from a paper is copying one point in a space, not the best point in it.

\section{What These Results Do Not Show}
\label{sec:notshown}

The measured effects are large, but their reach is narrow, and four limits are worth stating directly.

The effect was found on adherence, which is the measure that had room to move. Faithfulness and relevance sat near their ceilings in every condition, so this experiment cannot say whether rewording affects them. A failure to detect a change on a measure with a range of five points is not evidence that no change is there. Section~\ref{sec:limits} gives the size of effect this design could have found.

The effect rests on two of the three baselines. Adherence is undefined for baseline $A$, and this is worth stating plainly rather than leaving it as a note on a table. $A$ asks for one thing: \textit{use the following pieces of context to answer the question}. That is grounding, and grounding is what faithfulness measures. An adherence rule for $A$ would therefore be a second and cruder copy of a measure the design already has, and scoring it would count one failure twice and enter the two counts into the analysis as though they were independent. It would also flatter $A$: faithfulness runs near $0.95$, so a grounding rule would pass almost always and $A$ would appear to obey its prompt better than $B$ and $C$ on a rule it was never really tested against. The measure is therefore skipped rather than scored as satisfied, so that a condition never earns a free point for a requirement its prompt does not state.

The consequence is that the finding of Section~\ref{sec:effects} rests on baselines $B$ and $C$. Baseline $A$ is the shortest and plainest of the three prompts, and it is the one that cannot be tested on the measure where the whole effect appeared. Whether a prompt that asks for a single thing is as sensitive to rewording as one that asks for four is a question this design cannot answer, and it is not safe to assume the answer either way. The three rewordings of $A$ were still estimated on the other three measures, and none of them moved one that survives correction. That says less than it sounds, because those measures sat at their ceilings or on the floor. One piece of evidence points the other way. On the secondary F1 measure of Table~\ref{tab:f1}, the lexical rewording A1 lowers correctness in both cells, by $0.019$ at the gold level and $0.022$ at the retrieved level, and both intervals exclude zero. It is a small effect and it comes from a measure that is reported rather than decided on, so it settles nothing. It is enough to say that baseline $A$ should not be assumed immune.

The adherence rules are narrow proxies for what the prompts ask. Baseline $C$ asks the model to explain its choice, and the rule looks for a causal connective such as \textit{because} or \textit{therefore}. A connective is not an explanation. The count of markers reported above shows the rewordings genuinely stop the model from writing causal language, and not merely from writing one particular word, but whether the reasoning behind it is any good is not something this measure sees. The same holds for $B$, where a bracketed number is not a check that the cited passage supports the sentence. Faithfulness measures that separately, and it did not move.

Everything here is one generator, one judge, one corpus and one retrieval depth. The judge scored the two judged measures without a human agreement study behind it, as Section~\ref{sec:judge} notes, so those two measures carry no reliability estimate. The two measures the verdict rests on are program-checked and do not depend on the judge, which limits the damage, but the faithfulness result does depend on it.

The secondary correctness measure is not a measure of truth. Token F1 rewards overlapping words, so a fluent wrong answer that reuses the vocabulary of the question can score above a short correct one. It is a better proxy than containment on this data, which is a claim about containment, not a defence of F1.

\chapter{Conclusion}
\label{ch:conclusion}

\section{Summary of Findings}
\label{sec:summary}

This thesis asked how far systematic changes in the system prompt influence the behaviour and outcomes of a RAG system when the user input, the knowledge base, the retrieval mechanism and the underlying language model are held fixed. The answer is that they influence it a great deal, and that the size of the influence depends on which property of the answer is measured.

Twelve system prompts were built from three published baselines. Each baseline was written in four wordings: its canonical published form and three rewordings that change the vocabulary, the sentence structure or the layout. Every wording states the same requirements, so the only thing that varies across the four is $\varphi$. Each prompt was run over 100 MS MARCO queries at two evidence levels with three repetitions, which produced 6,768 answers, and every answer was scored on four measures.

The clearest result is on instruction adherence. Eleven of the twelve estimates exclude zero after correction, ten of them in both evidence levels, and the effects are large. Rewriting baseline $C$ in the passive voice costs it 46 points of adherence at the gold level, and rewriting it with synonyms costs 44. The requirements did not change in either case. Only the phrasing did.

The direction of the effect is not fixed. Every rewording of baseline $B$ scored higher than its published form, and every rewording of baseline $C$ scored lower. Rewording is therefore not a hazard to be guarded against but a parameter that can be set well or badly. It also follows that a prompt published with a system is not the best form of that prompt: three rewritings of baseline $B$, none of which changes what it asks for, all outperform the original.

On faithfulness and answer relevance no effect was found that survives correction, with one exception in one cell that does not replicate in the other. That is a weaker statement than it appears. Both measures sat near the top of their range in every condition, faithfulness never below $0.91$, so there was little room for a rewording to move them. This design could not have detected a small change there, and Section~\ref{sec:limits} gives the size it could have detected.

Correctness produced a result about measurement rather than about prompts. Measured by containment, the form fixed before the run, no estimate excludes zero. Measured by token F1 on the same answers with the same estimator, thirteen of eighteen do. Containment asks whether the reference answer appears inside the generated answer word for word, which floors on this data and leaves the measure with almost nothing to detect. Both are reported and the preregistered measure keeps its place in the decision. Where two measures of one property disagree this sharply, the disagreement describes the instruments, and a null from a measure with no room to move is not evidence of absence.

Taken together, $H_0$ is rejected. Prompts that state the same requirements in different words do not produce the same behaviour from a RAG system. The wording of a system prompt is not a cosmetic property of it.

\section{Contribution}
\label{sec:contribution}

The thesis makes four contributions.

The first is formal. Chapter 4 extends the formulation of \citet{lewis2020rag} so that the system prompt is an explicit conditioning variable of the generator, and then splits it into a requirement set and a wording, $S = \rho(\Sigma, \varphi)$. That split is what makes the question answerable. Without it, changing a prompt changes what it asks for and how it asks at the same time, and no difference in behaviour can be assigned to either. With it, the wording becomes an independent variable that can be varied on its own, the assignment of a condition becomes an intervention $\mathrm{do}(S = S_j)$, and the quantity of interest becomes an average treatment effect rather than an observed association.

The second is a design that holds the rest of the system fixed by construction rather than by assertion. Retrieval runs to completion and is written to disk before any prompt exists, so no execution path connects a prompt condition to what was retrieved. The context assembled for a query is drawn from a generator seeded on the query, the evidence level and the repetition, and never on the condition, so all twelve conditions receive an identical user turn. That property was checked on the collected data rather than assumed. The causal claim rests on these two facts, and both are enforced in code and tested.

The third is empirical. Prompt sensitivity has been shown for user prompts outside retrieval-augmented settings. This thesis measures it for the system prompt inside one, with the retriever, the corpus, the index and the generator held constant, and reports effects on instruction adherence that are large, bidirectional and replicated across two evidence levels. It also gives a practical result for anyone building such a system: the published form of a prompt is one point in a space of equivalent wordings, not the best point in it.

The fourth is methodological, and it was not planned. The preregistered correctness measure and a secondary measure of the same property, computed on the same answers, return opposite verdicts. Reporting both, and keeping the preregistered one in the decision, shows how far a conclusion about prompt sensitivity can depend on the instrument rather than on the system. Any study of this kind should check whether its measures have room to move before it reads a null as an absence of effect.

\section{Future Work}
\label{sec:futurework}

Several directions follow from the limits of what was done here.

The results rest on one generator. Whether a rewording that helps one model helps another, or whether the direction of the effect is a property of the model rather than of the wording, is open. Repeating the design across model families and sizes would settle it, and the design supports it: the generator is a single fixed parameter and nothing else would change.

The judged measures carry no reliability estimate. Section~\ref{sec:judge} specifies an agreement study against human labels, and the sampling for it is implemented, but the labelling was not carried out. Completing it would put a bound on how far the judge itself is sensitive to the surface form of the answers it grades, which matters here more than usual, since the thesis assumes models are sensitive to their instructions and the judge is a model.

The adherence measures are narrow. A bracketed number is not a check that the cited passage supports the sentence, and a causal connective is not an explanation. Both rules were chosen because a rule can decide them, and both are proxies for a requirement that is broader than the rule. Measures that check whether a citation is warranted, or whether a justification is sound, would test the same prompts against what they actually ask for.

The three rewording axes each change several things at once. The lexical rewording of baseline $C$ replaces many words, and the syntactic one changes voice, mood and clause order together. The data show that compliance falls furthest under the passive form, but the design cannot say that the passive voice is the cause, because it was not varied on its own. Varying one linguistic feature at a time would turn the observation in Section~\ref{sec:reading} into a claim.

If published prompts are not optimal, the natural next question is how to find better ones. Searching over wordings with the requirement set held fixed is a well-defined problem, and the estimator used here already provides its objective.

Finally, the query set is short factoid web search. Section~\ref{sec:usecase} motivates retrieval with settings such as clinical decision support and legal question answering, whose documents differ from web passages in length, structure and register. Whether an instruction that governs behaviour on MS MARCO governs it there is untested, and the effects reported here bound what may be expected in those settings without establishing it.


